 "e0146": "In grammatical terminology, German actually retained the Latin vocabulary more thoroughly than English did: where English replaced the Latin terms with native or French-derived alternatives, namely ~tense~ (from Old French ~tens~), ~mood~ (remodeled from ~mode~ after Old English ~mōd~), and ~voice~ (from Old French ~vois~), German kept the Latin originals: ~Tempus~, ~Modus~, ~Genus Verbi~.",
